@riverar we get the information from Windows. .NET doesn't carry any globalization data. When setting your default region formats to English (Europ), Windows assign this to a locale called en-150 and that will return the `150` as the 2 letters ISO region code. It looks ICU define this locale same http://www.localeplanet.com/icu/en-150/index.html. I don't think Europ by itself has region code and that is why they used 150 to point at Europ and use 001 for international (i.e. en-001). The question here, if you choose English (Europ), what code you expect to get? `NL` is the wrong answer anyway. 

If you still feel this is wrong, you may log an issue to the Windows team through the Feedback Hub on Windows 10. 

the [article ](https://www.ctrl.blog/entry/en-001.html)  maybe a useful to read too.

